{#
    The rail: the pages of the section the reader is in, not every page there
    is.

    The bar picks the section — Guidelines, Frontend, the design system — and
    this is what is inside the one that was picked. Rendering the whole tree
    put the guidelines under a page about the frontend, which is a sitemap
    rather than navigation.

    Which section that is has to be worked out rather than asked for:
    `isCurrent` and `isInRootline` are not set on these nodes, so the signal is
    the link the renderer resolves to `#` — its way of saying "the page you are
    on". A section is the one whose entry, or one of whose children, resolves
    to that.

    A section with no pages under it leaves the rail listing the sections
    themselves, which is the only useful thing left to say.

    **The rail is given its list, not built out of one.** `sds-rail` takes
    entries as data — that is its documented form, and a group is
    `{ label, items }` — so what this file produces is the list and nothing
    else. It used to write `.sds-rail__item` and `.sds-rail__group` and the
    `<details>` around them, which is the element's own markup spelt in a
    second place: the fold, the chevron, the active mark and where `aria-current`
    goes were all stated twice and could only ever agree by hand.

    Which one is current travels as an index, because that is what the rail
    counts in — one number over the flattened list, groups included.
#}
{%- set here = node.menuEntries|filter(entry =>
        renderLink(entry.url) == '#'
        or entry.children|filter(child => renderLink(child.url) == '#')|length > 0
    )|first -%}
{%- set inside = here and here.children|length > 0 -%}

{%- set items = [] -%}
{%- set active = -1 -%}
{%- set flat = 0 -%}

{%- if inside -%}
    {%- for entry in here.children -%}
        {%- if entry.children|length -%}
            {#- A page with pages under it is a group. Its own link is the first
                item inside the fold rather than the summary, because a summary
                that is also a link is a control with two jobs and the fold
                wins the press. -#}
            {%- set group = [] -%}
            {%- set url = renderLink(entry.url) -%}
            {%- if url == '#' %}{% set active = flat %}{% endif -%}
            {%- set group = group|merge([{ label: entry.value.toString, href: url }]) -%}
            {%- set flat = flat + 1 -%}
            {%- for child in entry.children -%}
                {%- set curl = renderLink(child.url) -%}
                {%- if curl == '#' %}{% set active = flat %}{% endif -%}
                {%- set group = group|merge([{ label: child.value.toString, href: curl }]) -%}
                {%- set flat = flat + 1 -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
            {%- set items = items|merge([{
                label: entry.value.toString,
                items: group,
                open: entry.isInRootline
            }]) -%}
        {%- else -%}
            {%- set url = renderLink(entry.url) -%}
            {%- if url == '#' %}{% set active = flat %}{% endif -%}
            {%- set items = items|merge([{ label: entry.value.toString, href: url }]) -%}
            {%- set flat = flat + 1 -%}
        {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
{%- else -%}
    {#- The sections themselves, one line each. Their pages belong to them and
        appear when the reader is inside one — a rail that unfolded every
        section at once would be the sitemap again. -#}
    {%- for entry in node.menuEntries -%}
        {%- set url = renderLink(entry.url) -%}
        {%- if url == '#' %}{% set active = flat %}{% endif -%}
        {%- set items = items|merge([{ label: entry.value.toString, href: url }]) -%}
        {%- set flat = flat + 1 -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
{%- endif -%}

<sds-rail
    label="{{ inside ? here.value.toString : '' }}"
    items="{{ items|json_encode }}"
    {%- if active >= 0 %} active="{{ active }}"{% endif -%}
></sds-rail>
